' Sibling of choice-prompt-render.puml, which owns the gating logic and the prose.
' This file owns only the resolved layout: what the operator confronts once
' applicableRows and gateAddons have already executed.
'
' The checkboxes are grouped into two PLANES, each named by its own divider:
' execution (where the agent runs) and interface (what it can drive). One
' undifferentiated "add-ons" row put a Docker daemon beside a browser as though
' they answered the same question, and named only the first group because the
' divider was inferred from "the first multi-select row" rather than declared.
' Source: internal/choiceui/choiceui.go draw(); internal/run/choices.go row builders.
' AMENDED 2026-11-13 (owner, O7): FOUR SECTIONS, in question order: agent
' (credentials, model), execution (OS, trust repo), reach (egress, mcp) and
' interface (devices, outputs, ports, apps). A stub names each one, not a
' centred divider (ONE CARD, below).
'
' The execution group includes trust repo directly below OS. It defaults to
' off. Its current opt-in runs mise trust on workspace-root config files before
' the agent starts. On permits project-defined mise behavior, so the risk axis
' places on left of off. The MCP gateway row follows the same on-left,
' off-right order: enabling it grants an agent more integrations.
' Off skips new trust; it does not revoke a prior mise trust record in the
' durable tool home, including one the operator made outside proveo.
'
' Credentials now lives in execution below trust repo: disabled API keys,
' OAuth and SSH checkboxes. Populated cells carry the ports-style arrow and
' expose a full read-only list. Empty cells have neither a tick nor an arrow.
' The host inventory never changes transport or billing selections.
' See _spec/cmd/proveo/github-access.puml for inventory and transport contracts.
' AMENDED 2026-11-13 (owner, O7 and O4): credentials opens the agent section,
' above model, and gains a fourth class, .env (THE .env CELL, below).
'
' Salt possesses no conditional construct. Optionality is consequently encoded,
' never branched — the five encodings are demonstrated below and named in the
' legend. The variant that eliminates the form entirely (no TTY) has no frame.
'
' The models row shows the BRIDGE OUTPUT, not the role vars the operator set.
' defs/claudecode/mcp/entrypoint.sh maps ARCHITECT_MODEL (falling back to
' EDITOR_MODEL) into ANTHROPIC_MODEL, and SMALL_MODEL into
' ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL — two slots, so a third role var is never read.
' Bridge dialects for the other harnesses: _spec/internal/entrypoint/model-alias-bridges.puml.
'
' NOT drawn here, and all of it shipped: the topology strip under the hints, and
' the three regions the form is now painted in — a droppable head, a scrolling
' body indented two columns for its gutter, and a foot that follows the body.
'   the mechanism ..... _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-strip.puml
'   the regions ....... _spec/internal/choiceui/viewport.puml
'   every frame ....... _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-states.puml
'   still unbuilt ..... _spec/internal/choiceui/figure-fidelities.puml (the side pane)
' This file stays the resolved layout of one screenful, at a size that holds it.
' AMENDED 2026-11-13: the body sits inside the card, and its gutter rides the
' card's right wall instead of two columns on the left (ONE CARD, below).
'
' ── 2026-10-05: EXECUTION'S ROW IS "OS"; "ports" JOINS IT ─────────────────
' (Moved 2026-11-13 from a note that sat after @endsalt, where no renderer
' read it; the text is unchanged.)
' The divider still reads "execution"; its row label is "OS" (host ·
' sandbox). The interface divider stays; its row label is "devices" (tui ·
' browser · android · ios). Below trust repo and credentials, sbx only, a
' multi-select "ports" row offers each discovered dev port, all unticked.
' Past four, column 4 is "...More" and a dropdown lists the rest.
' _spec/internal/devports/dev-ports.puml
' AMENDED 2026-11-13 (owner, O7): ports and apps sit under interface, after
' outputs. The dropdown is now the open list, a small card (ONE CARD).

' ── ONE CARD (2026-11-13) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
' The owner chose the One Card look after two rounds of mocks (O1). The
' reversal of "no sbx cards" is recorded in wizard-keys.puml. Every wizard page
' is one rounded card: this form, init's forms, the scope page and the checks
' page. The owner fixed what the card says (O1-O8). The Executor fixed its
' geometry (E1-E16, F1, F2) and the choices made while building it. card.go
' draws the frame for every page.
'
' WIDTH (Executor, E1). The card is cw = min(w, 120) columns, walls included
' (cardMax). It draws its frame when w >= 40 (cardMin). Under 40 it draws no
' walls: text starts at column 0, as before the card. The rows the frame
' would take stay blank there, so one ladder serves both. Dialogs and the
' open list are content-sized cards (confirm-dialogs.puml; THE OPEN LIST).
'
' COLUMNS INSIDE A FRAMED CARD (Executor, E3):
'   0        the left wall; a section's stub starts here
'   2        the title, the "›" row marker, the key line, the help
'   4        row labels, section names, the facts
'   18       the grid's column 0 (optOrigin)
'   cw-2     the inner edge: every write is cut before it
'   cw-1     the right wall; the scroll marks ride it
' The walls paint AFTER the body text, so overflow shows as cut text and
' never hides behind a wall. The canvas also clips body rows at cw-2.
'
' THE HEAD (Executor, E5). Row 0 is the top border ╭─╮. Row 1 holds the
' title, bold, at column 2. On a wizard page a step chip sits at the right:
' " page n/m " here, " scope · n/m " and " checks · n/m " on the other
' pages. The chip is a fill (brand ink on ColorSurface) that ends at cw-3, so
' column cw-2 stays blank. The banner, when the ladder buys it, sits above
' the card.
' THE CHIP OWNS THE PAGE COUNT (owner, O6). While the chip draws, "page n/m"
' leaves the key line. One predicate, stepChip, decides it for draw() and for
' the plain hint() that tests and e2e read, so the two cannot disagree.
'   it draws       when title + 2 + chip fit in cw-4 (framed), or when
'                  title + 1 + chip fit in w (unframed: the threshold the
'                  title row used before the card)
'   it also draws  when the whole title does not fit, the key line cannot
'                  keep "page n/m" whole, and 8 title runes plus "…" still
'                  fit; the title is then cut with … before the chip
'   otherwise      no chip; "page n/m" stays on the key line at keep rank 5
' THE STEP COUNT NEVER VANISHES WHERE IT SHOWED BEFORE THE CARD (Executor, F1
' and review fix 4). Under 40 columns the chip draws flush right on the title
' row when it fits, as before. Framed and without a chip, the page count
' ranks 5, above "N more below" (4).
' MEASURED by review: the checks page at 40 columns with a 22-rune title lost
' its count in the head and in the key line. The cut-title rule fixed it:
' "checks before the r…   checks · 1/4".
' [Inference] "N more below" ranks below the page count, so the hidden-row
' count never changes the chip decision; stepChip therefore reads
' keyParts(0, …).
'
' THE FACTS (Executor, E6 and F2). A header line that reads "key: value"
' (putHeader's colon test) is a fact. Facts flow on one row from column 4,
' joined by four spaces, and wrap at a fact boundary to width cw-6. Any other
' header line keeps its own row. An indented line (a NAME=value setting, a
' GitHub fingerprint) starts at the facts column too: column 4 framed, 2
' unframed. A fact too long for the row clips with …. No blank row follows
' the facts. The ladder and the paint both call Form.facts(w), so the ladder
' charges exactly the rows the paint uses. The header rung still drops the
' facts as a whole.
' THE SETTINGS-NAMES LINE IS GONE (owner, O3). The header no longer prints a
' line naming the loaded settings whose values it hides (CODE_THEME,
' DARK_MODE); EnvHeader skips them. "keys:" and the NAME=value model settings
' stay. The cut is in choiceui.EnvHeader, not in run's buildHeader: the same
' loadedSettings map also feeds the model NAME=value lines.
'
' SECTIONS (Executor, E7). A Divider row draws a one-line stub "├── name ──"
' from the left wall, with the name at column 4. The stub runs max(16,
' len(name) + 7) columns, so a name longer than nine grows it instead of
' being cut. Only a name that would reach the inner edge clips, with ….
' A stub costs ONE body line where the blank, centred heading and blank cost
' three, so a divider row costs two lines (viewport.puml).
' The cursor's section lights: its stub rule and the left-wall cells beside
' its rows draw in the rail style (brand, not bold), and its name draws bold.
' Other stubs, walls and names draw in the frame style (grey). Rows above the
' first divider form a section of their own. The head, the facts, Skip and
' the key line never light; with the cursor on Skip nothing lights.
' Unframed, a stub "──" starts at column 1, so the gutter at column 0 never
' covers it.
' A row whose stub scrolled off takes its label back (headingsInWindow). E7
' expected that code to become unreachable. It did not: rows other than the
' cursor's still reach it (MEASURED at 80x12), so the Executor kept it.
'
' THE FOOT (Executor, E8). One join rule ├─┤, the key line at column 2, then
' the bottom border ╰─╯. The Skip button, when the form names one, draws
' inside the walls above the foot rule, and the ladder charges its two rows
' there. cardRows = 5 (top border, title, foot rule, key line, bottom border)
' is the card's fixed cost: the ladder's base is cardRows + body + help.
'
' SCROLL MARKS RIDE THE RIGHT WALL (Executor, E9). ▲ and ▼ replace the wall's
' cell in the frame style; the ░ thumb draws in the rail style. Under the
' ASCII and Off tiers they are ^, v and a # thumb, so the thumb differs from
' the | wall. The left wall stays free for the section light. Unframed, the
' gutter is column 0.
'
' THE GRID'S PITCH (Executor, E4). pitch = clamp((cw - 32) / last, 16, 22).
' last is the largest pinned column on the form (3 on the run form), and 32
' is optOrigin plus the last cell's 14 columns. With no column pinned above 0
' the pitch stays 22 (colPitch). At 80 columns the grid sits at 18, 34, 50,
' 66; at 120 it sits at 18, 40, 62, 84. A cell clips to min(pitch - 2, room
' to the edge), its label first, with … before its ▾.
' MEASURED (final check): the .env cell at column 3 is absent at 69 columns
' and less, draws [x] .… without its ▾ from 70 to 76, [x] .e… ▾ at 77, and
' whole from 78; its list still opens when the cursor reaches it.
'
' UNDER THE CARD (Executor, E10), in this order: the help (column 2, wrapping
' at cw-4), the topology figure or its digest caption, then "on enter"
' (heading at 2, numbers at 4, text at 7; unframed 0, 2, 5). The pane draws
' the figure beside the card only when w >= cw + 2 + 80: 202 columns beside
' a full card.
' A help line that opens with "off:" or "always on:" draws that head bold and
' the rest in the terminal's ink; lime left the help and the option cells.
' MEASURED by review: help that wraps at cw-4 can take one more line than it
' did at the terminal's width, so at height 9 some wide terminals now report
' "terminal too small" where the form drew before the card.
'
' THE PAINT ORDER. The head and chip; the facts rows; the body text, cut at
' cw-2; the walls and scroll marks over it; Skip; the foot rule; the key
' line; the bottom border; the help; the figure; on enter; and the open list
' LAST, so nothing paints over it.
'
' THE LEGEND IS GONE (owner, O2). No form draws "◀ riskier … safer ▶": the
' run form and init's forms lost it, and with it Form.NoAxis, axisLabel and
' the ladder's axis rung (Executor, E15). The owner: "remove the
' riskier/safer tags, only leave reach". The columns keep their risk order.
'
' THE RUN FORM ASKS IN THIS ORDER (owner, O7; design R1 "Questions in order"):
'   agent       credentials, model (when sourceRow draws one)
'   execution   OS, trust repo
'   reach       egress, mcp (each when applicableRows keeps it)
'   interface   devices, outputs, ports (when discovered), apps (when
'               discovered)
' run.section(name, rows) marks the first present row of each section, and a
' section with no rows draws no stub. Labels and options did not change.
' run.runRows builds the rows without a terminal, so a table test checks the
' order per harness. The first section is "agent", as O7 names it; the
' design panel's "account" was not applied.
' THE CURSOR OPENS ON THE TOP ROW WITH AN ANSWER TO CHANGE (owner, O8):
' choice-prompt-render.puml (WHERE THE CURSOR MAY REST). On claudecode with
' no key and no login that is model · API keys (MEASURED, review step 9).
'
' THE .env CELL (owner, O4). credentials gains a fourth class after SSH:
' ".env", in grid column 3. Its list holds the names the run's env file sets
' (p.HostEnvFile, so PROVEO_ENV_FILE wins when set). The list holds names
' only, A to Z ignoring case, read-only, with no tick boxes (More.Bare). With
' no names the cell draws "[ ] .env" with no ▾ and opens nothing. The names
' come from credentials.EnvFileNames, which wraps the one parser:
' _spec/internal/credentials/credential-decisions.puml (ONE PARSER).
' THE FILTER (owner, O4). "/" opens it. Typed letters narrow the list by
' case-insensitive subsequence; backspace deletes one rune; ctrl+u empties
' the query. While it types, every printable key is text, q j k h l and
' space included. ENTER CLOSES THE FILTER: typing stops, the matches stay,
' and the form is NOT accepted. Esc while typing clears the query and stops
' typing; Esc with a query left standing clears it; otherwise Esc is the
' page's. Leaving the cell drops the query. The keys: wizard-keys.puml. The
' matcher: scope-tree.puml (THREE MATCHERS).
' Backspace on an empty query keeps the filter typing, while the scope page's
' filter closes there. O4 named neither; the Executor kept typing on.
'
' THE OPEN LIST IS A SMALL CARD (Executor, E13), painted last.
'   width     max(28, widest entry + 6, name + count + 11), shifted left so
'             it ends at or before cw-2; under 7 columns (listFloor) it
'             draws nothing
'   column    an entry's tick and name start in the cell's own column
'   top       "╭── <cell> ─ N above ── <count> ─╮": the count reads "12",
'             or "5 of 12" while a query stands; "N above" shows when the
'             window hides entries above it and room allows
'   filter    a filterable list's first line: "/ filter" in grey at rest,
'             "/ <query>_" while typing; a long query shows its tail
'   entries   "›" on the cursor's entry, which takes the answer fill (grey
'             on a read-only list, cyan on the ports list); matched letters
'             bold; "nothing matches" when no entry does
'   bottom    "╰── N more ──╯", counting only the entries below the window
'   height    fixed when it opens, from the whole list, so narrowing never
'             moves the box
' PLACEMENT. Inside the card, on the side of its row with more rows between
' the head and the foot rule, when that side holds min(3, n) entries. Else
' under the bottom border, over the help and the figure. Else inside with
' whatever fits. Else nowhere. It never covers the title, its own row, the
' foot rule, the key line or the bottom border.
' MEASURED by review before placement landed: painted last with the old
' placement, the list covered the key line, and "enter accept" vanished at
' 734 width x height combinations of the lists-open fixture.
' Under the card the list can cut a help line mid-word ("publish this por").
' E13 allows that; nobody has asked the owner whether the help should win.
' PER-LIST HELP (Executor). More.Help answers first, then Row.Help, then
' "publish this port". Each list carries its own map, so a .env name never
' collides with another list's entry or a cell's help. An API keys, OAuth or
' SSH entry reads "› <name> — configured on the host; read-only". A .env
' entry reads "› <NAME> — set in <~/path>; this list shows names only". The
' .env cell reads "N name(s) in <~/path>; read-only; this list shows names
' only", or "no .env file for this run".
' NOT "the value is never shown" (Executor, review fix 2). The header can
' print NAME=value for a model setting the same .env sets (ARCHITECT_MODEL),
' so that sentence was false on one screen. The list's own claim is true.
'
' STATE STYLES (owner, O5). Every answer draws its state, and a fill marks
' the cursor's answer instead of an underline:
'   focus     the cursor's answer: black on brand; bold when chosen
'   focusOff  the cursor on a fixed or unavailable answer: black on grey,
'             struck through when unavailable
'   chosen    an answer in force that the operator chose: bold
'   offered   an answer not chosen: grey (#8A8A8A)
'   fixed     an answer in force the operator cannot change: plain ink
'   gated     an unavailable answer: faint and struck through
'   locked    a locked row's label: faint; the cursor's row keeps brand
' A locked row, or an Off cell, draws its answer in force as fixed and the
' rest as gated. A text field keeps its underline. Body text lost its fixed
' #E5E4E4 ink, which vanished on a white terminal, and draws in the
' terminal's own ink. Every fill is Reverse plus explicit colours, so
' NO_COLOR keeps a visible face (tui-design-language.puml, ONE CARD).
' The legend maps salt's encodings onto these styles: salt grey is NOT the
' terminal's grey.
'
